  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nTHUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nTODAY...The next 24 hours presents a low-confidence forecast, with \nthe location, timing, and coverage of thunderstorms uncertain. For \ntoday...an MCV moving east-southeast across western into central KS \namidst an increasingly unstable airmass may support a few showers \nand thunderstorms throughout the day across portions of central and \neastern KS. If this scenario plays out, strong instability in \nconcert with marginal deep layer shear may support a few strong to \nsevere thunderstorms, especially this afternoon. Not expecting \nwidespread coverage with this activity, so most locations should \nstay thunderstorm-free. \n\nTHIS EVENING--EARLY WEDNESDAY...Two areas of thunderstorms look to \ndevelop later today...1) across Nebraska and far northern KS ahead \nof an approaching weak cold front, and 2) the High Plains of far \nwestern KS and eastern CO with an upslope flow regime. This activity \nmay congeal into one or more thunderstorm complexes with time and \ntrack southeast this evening and tonight into portions of central \nand eastern KS. Thinking the greatest hail threat will be early-on \nwith convection northwest of the forecast area, with the primary \nthreat being 50-70 mph winds further southeast through about the KS \nTurnpike, given the hefty downdraft CAPE, especially if \nthunderstorms congeal and grow upscale. Activity could linger into \nWednesday morning over mainly south-central and southeast KS.\n\nSUNDAY--TUESDAY...The upper ridge finally shifts east from the \nsouthwest to the south-central CONUS by this weekend, as shortwave \nenergy tracks across the northern/western CONUS and southern Canada. \nThis should allow a weak cold front to move in from the north, with \nthe potential for increasing thunderstorm chances late Sunday \nthrough at least Tuesday.\n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nWith northwest flow aloft persisting across the region and the \nassociated influence of Canadian high pressure oozing in from the \nnorth, near to slightly above average temperatures are expected \nthrough late week, with forecast highs mostly in the upper 80s \nthrough mid-90s, warmest over southern Kansas. the \"coolest\" days \nwill likely be Wednesday and Thursday in wake of tonight's weak cool \nfront. As we head toward this weekend, the stout upper ridge should \nshift east from the southwest CONUS to the south-central CONUS, \nsupporting a warming trend over the weekend into at least early next \nweek. Could be looking at another round of widespread 100s, although \nat least dewpoints should be lower and south-southwest winds on the \nbreezy side.",
